# The PI's Natural Wave Equation
## A Physics Model Without Negative or Imaginary Numbers

By banning imaginary numbers ($i$) and stripping away the fiction of negative numbers ($-$), we can replace abstract coordinate lines with absolute, positive physical measurements paired with spatial directions. 

Instead of subtracting, our system tracks wave dynamics using a rhythmic, physical handoff of energy between **four strictly positive tracking meters**.

---

## 1. The Variables (Pure, Positive Quantities)

The state of our guitar string at any position $x$ is defined by four meters that are always greater than or equal to zero ($u, v \ge 0$):

| Variable | What It Tracks | Physical Meaning |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| $u_{\text{up}}$ | **Upward Stretch** | How many inches the string is stretched **Up** from the center line. |
| $u_{\text{down}}$ | **Downward Stretch** | How many inches the string is stretched **Down** from the center line. |
| $v_{\text{up}}$ | **Upward Velocity** | The speed of the string moving **Up** (in inches per second). |
| $v_{\text{down}}$ | **Downward Velocity** | The speed of the string moving **Down** (in inches per second). |

---

## 2. The New System of Equations

Because we cannot use minus signs, we write our system as a set of **balancing instructions**. If a force wants to push the string down, it doesn't subtract from $v_{\text{up}}$—it adds directly to the $v_{\text{down}}$ meter.

### Rule A: The Height Shift (Tracking Position)
* **If** $v_{\text{up}} > 0 \implies$ Increase $u_{\text{up}}$
* **If** $v_{\text{down}} > 0 \implies$ Increase $u_{\text{down}}$

> **The Rule:** Upward speed feeds the Upward stretch; Downward speed feeds the Downward stretch.

### Rule B: The Tension Tug (Tracking Velocity)
Curvature naturally tries to pull the string back to the center line:
* $\text{Curvature of } u_{\text{up}} \implies$ Increase $v_{\text{down}}$
* $\text{Curvature of } u_{\text{down}} \implies$ Increase $v_{\text{up}}$

> **The Rule:** A sharp curve pointing Up creates a physical acceleration Down. A sharp curve pointing Down creates a physical acceleration Up.

